We can add the user-type as part of this interface and that will allow us to split the data.

{noformat}export interface OrderHistoryQuery {
    company_id: number;
    user_type?: ClaimUserType | null;
    user_id?: number | null;
    group_id?: number | null;
    from_timestamp?: number | null;
    to_timestamp?: number | null;
    page?: number | null;
    items_per_page?: number | null;
}{noformat}

We have to be attempt because this interface is shared between more than 1 endpoint, and that is also used for delivery, that statically adds the user_type for the filters.
It should not be a problem once the claim type for delivery is not available for Ride Booker rides, but we can also just ensure it does not use the data from the query, or ideally we can avoid reusing this interface

{noformat}private deliveryOrdersFilterExpr({
        user_id: userId,
        group_id: groupId,
        from_timestamp: fromTs,
        to_timestamp: toTs
    }: OrderHistoryQuery): DbSqlQuery[] {
        const clauses: DbSqlQuery[] = [];
        if (userId) {
            clauses.push(sql`
                AND food_claim.business_user_id = ${userId}
                AND food_claim.user_type = ${ClaimUserType.BUSINESS_USER}
            `);
        }
....{noformat}

On the rides filter, on the other hand, we must remove the static user type and be able to work with user ids and user types for the filtering, so we can support filtering by Ride Booker rides and Agents


{noformat}private ridesFilterExpr({
        user_type: userType,
        user_id: userId,
        group_id: groupId,
        from_timestamp: fromTs,
        to_timestamp: toTs
    }: OrderHistoryQuery): DbSqlQuery[] {
        const clauses: DbSqlQuery[] = [];
        if (userType) {
            clauses.push(sql`AND claim.user_type = ${userType}`);
        }
        if (userId) {
            clauses.push(sql`
                AND claim.business_user_id = ${userId}
            `);
        }
....{noformat}

On the business portal side, for the existing endpoints that are working working with Rides, we just make sure the user_type is static:

{noformat}private async getFullRidesData(
        ctx: Context,
        businessCompanyId: tBusinessCompanyId,
        {
            user_id: userId,
            group_id: groupId,
            start_date: fromTimestamp,
            end_date: toTimestamp,
            items_per_page: itemsPerPageInput,
            page
        }: RidesHistoryByRangeQuery,
        hardLimit: number | null
    ): Promise<PaginatedRidesData> {
        const itemsPerPage = itemsPerPageInput && hardLimit ? Math.min(itemsPerPageInput, hardLimit) : hardLimit;
        const ridesHistory = await this.businessInvoicingPortalApi.getRidesHistory(ctx, {
            company_id: businessCompanyId,
            user_type: BusinessInvoicingPortalInternalAsyncApi.ClaimUserType.BUSINESS_USER,
            user_id: userId,
            group_id: groupId,
            items_per_page: itemsPerPage,
            page: page,
            from_timestamp: fromTimestamp,
            to_timestamp: toTimestamp
        });
....{noformat}



